<p>Collapsed for ease of scrolling.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>With your response and update, I’m afraid I’ve formulated further questions regarding your concept.</p>
<p>To start with, considering the individuals in the center of the earth wouldn’t be able to see anything, how would they (and subsequently the Foundation) know where they were? I’m rather sure geolocation is harder when you’re literally at the center of the earth; however, that’s something else that could be hand-waved away as ‘co-opted anomalous tech’.</p>
<p>Speaking of anomalous tech, this line confused me a bit:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A note at the end describes how the foundation isn't pursuing this avenue of research due to logistics and a lack of benefits.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If the Foundation isn’t pursuing a way to get people back from the core of the earth, then what exactly are the people on the surface doing while they’re receiving these messages?</p>
<p>I’m also going to ask what may sound like a bit of a weird question: why the center of the earth? It seems like any inhospitable plane of existence could be substituted in with minimal change to the plot. This tends to be a bad sign, in my experience. To give an example of this kind of problem, let’s consider the movies <em>Toy Story</em> and <em>Cars</em>. The problems faced by the protagonists in <em>Toy Story</em> (getting replaced, getting lost, etc.) are problems that only toys could possibly have, while the problems in <em>Cars</em> (getting stranded, losing the ‘big race’) are completely separate from the movie’s protagonist being a living car. As a consequence, the narrative of <em>Toy Story</em> feels considerably more streamlined and straightforward than <em>Cars</em>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

